# Keep this repo deployment-focused: include only runtime files.
*

!.gitignore
!.dockerignore
!index.html
!Dockerfile
!render.yaml
!vercel.json
!.vercelignore
!DEPLOY_ONLINE.md
!package.json
!build.js
!multiplayer_server.py
!snap_score.py
!fish_game_all_in_one.py
!tournament_engine.py
!tournament_server.py
# Clan System: server-authoritative clans core (seasons, points, roles, chat)
# and its unit + integration tests (in-memory Firestore fake), plus the client
# module smoke test (end-game claim dedup, bridge off-switch).
!clan_server.py
!test_clan_server.py
!test_clans_ui.js

# Prestige (descend past Level 100). multiplayer_server.py imports this at module
# scope, so if the allowlist ever drops it the server dies on startup with an
# ImportError instead of failing soft. It owns no level curve of its own, the
# cap and progress fn are injected from LEVEL_XP_TOTALS via prestige_server.init.
!prestige_server.py
# Its tests: the reward math + the whole commit transaction against an
# in-memory Firestore fake, plus the two tables that MUST mirror the client
# (the avatar relock map and the skinnable-animal roster): those two checks
# are the only thing standing between "a new avatar was added" and "a bought
# avatar gets relocked by a Prestige".
!test_prestige_server.py
!test_prestige_ui.js

# The admin XP tool (/api/admin/set_xp): sets one account's lifetime XP to an
# exact number and rewrites every level field derived from it in the same write.
# The test pins the derivation AND that the server's level table still matches
# the client's: the two are what keep the leaderboard, header and profile
# telling the same story about an account.
!test_admin_set_xp.py

# Developer Analytics (admin-only dashboard). multiplayer_server.py imports this
# at module scope too, so dropping it from the allowlist kills the server on
# startup rather than just hiding the dashboard. Read-only: it derives every
# number from the accounts and the game records this server already writes, and
# exposes no endpoint that mutates anything.
!analytics_server.py
# Its tests: the metric maths against fixed fake data (so a wrong average or an
# off-by-one date window fails loudly), the admin gate, and the "a rate with
# nothing under it is unknown, not 0%" rule that keeps the dashboard honest.
!test_analytics_server.py
# The client half: the REAL renderers driven with REAL server payloads, which is
# what stops the two sides drifting while both look green on their own.
!test_analytics_ui.js

# Newsletter. multiplayer_server.py imports newsletter_server at module scope and
# newsletter_server imports newsletter_email, so BOTH have to be on the allowlist:
# ship one without the other and the container dies on startup with an
# ImportError that takes the whole game down, not just the mailing list.
# .dockerignore and the Dockerfile
# COPY lines carry the same pair; all three have to agree.
!newsletter_server.py
!newsletter_email.py

# Discord join reward. multiplayer_server.py imports this at module scope, so
# dropping it from the allowlist kills the whole server on startup with an
# ImportError, not just the reward. It holds the OAuth round-trip that proves a
# player is really in the Discord server, and the transaction that pays the
# Critter Coins exactly once per game account AND once per Discord account.
!discord_server.py
# Its tests: the "cannot be paid twice" guarantee from both directions, the
# signed OAuth state (forged/expired/replayed), and the rule that no failure of
# any kind, Discord down, scope missing, garbled reply, is ever mistaken for
# membership. Plus the client chip's own render/claim rules.
!test_discord_rewards.py
!test_discord_reward_ui.js

# Level Pass (the free reward track over levels 1–100). multiplayer_server.py
# imports this at module scope, so dropping it from the allowlist kills the
# whole server on startup with an ImportError, not just the pass. It holds the
# track definition and the transaction that pays each tier EXACTLY once, after
# re-deriving the account's level from its own stored total_xp.
!level_pass_server.py
# Its tests: the "cannot be claimed twice" guarantee, the level gate that
# ignores whatever the client claimed its level was, the consumable caps, and
# the rule that a tier which cannot pay (no backgrounds left) writes no ledger
# entry so it stays claimable later.
!test_level_pass_server.py
# The client half: the REAL level-pass renderer against REAL server payloads,
# plus the avatar-gallery level track and the XP-boost multiplier.
!test_level_pass_ui.js

# Friend-code referral reward. Module-scope import again, same consequence if
# it goes missing. It pays TWO accounts in one transaction, which is why none
# of it can live in the browser.
!referral_server.py
# Its tests: both payouts landing together, the once-ever-per-account guard,
# the self-referral / mutual-referral / stale-window refusals, and the
# every-fifth-friend background going to the REFERRER only.
!test_referral_server.py
!test_referral_ui.js
# The Discord Developer Portal walkthrough for switching the reward on.
!DISCORD_REWARD_SETUP.md

# Clans: headless-Chrome render check, every screen against the REAL css/module
# (it caught a season countdown that never started).
!test_clans_render.js
# Clans: the server↔client seam, payloads from the REAL clan_server rendered by
# the REAL clans-ui.js, so the two halves can't drift apart while both are green.
!test_clans_payloads.py
# Clans: every one of the 51 clan challenges, driven to completion one step at a
# time, and proved not to fire a step early, or to fire anything else by
# accident. Also covers the telemetry multiplayer_server derives to feed them.
!test_clan_challenges.py
!test_tournament_engine.py
!test_tournament_server.py
!test_tournament_integration.py
!test_deck_shuffle_fairness.py
# Card symbols: reads the symbol back OUT of the printed badge on every
# card sheet, because nothing else ties cards_*.txt to the art and a
# rebuild silently left nine cards describing the wrong symbol.
!test_card_symbols_match_art.py
# Trading: the server-side swap core, its end-to-end flow, and the client rule
# that makes a traded-away item be earned again instead of re-granted for free.
!test_trade_logic.py
!test_trade_integration.py
!test_avatar_reearn.js
# Messages: chat-background docs share the messages subcollection with group
# rosters, so this pins down that they never get mistaken for one another.
!test_chat_backgrounds.js
# Chat mute silences NOTIFICATIONS ONLY, badges and dots, while messages keep
# arriving and unread counters keep counting. Zeroing a counter instead of
# hiding a badge would make everything received while muted look read.
!test_chat_mute.js
# Undo pressed during the BOTS' turns. The unit half drives the real
# submit_undo/_apply_pending_undo_restore against a stub; the integration half
# spins up a REAL GameRoom and a real match and presses Undo mid bot-think.
!test_undo_fix.py
!test_undo_integration.py
# Bracket builder: the design logic (checked against the Python validator) and
# the headless-Chrome geometry check for the connection lines.
!test_tournament_builder.js
!test_tournament_builder_check.py
!test_tournament_builder_lines.js
# Tournament: being knocked out must not cut you off from watching the rest of
# the bracket, pins the "out" vs "done" split and the Spectate-button rules.
!test_tournament_spectate.js
# Board: headless-Chrome check that a tall board of oceans can actually be
# scrolled to its bottom, on a phone-sized viewport as well as a laptop.
!test_board_scroll.js
# Board peek: the hover preview of another player's board must never get stuck
# on screen, a removed anchor fires no mouseleave, so every dismissal path
# (pointer off, click, scroll, tab away, game over, leaving the game) is pinned.
!test_board_peek_dismiss.js
# Stars: every ★ ability fired for real through the engine, plus the payload
# contract behind the gold "discard this to fire the ★" payment border.
!test_star_abilities.py
# Stars: the gold border rule itself, incl. computed colours in headless Chrome.
!test_star_gold_border.js
# Clownfish: the one card whose ability is whatever Ocean it sits on. Pins both
# halves, it counts as one more of that Ocean without scoring its chart twice,
# and on a Mangrove or an Arctic Ocean it carries that Ocean's ★ (play again),
# which is the half the engine did not do.
!test_clownfish_copy.py
# Discarding down to the hand limit: the trim removes exactly the cards you
# picked and draws NOTHING back (a board ★ used to refill the hand as fast as
# you emptied it), and clicking a fanned hand card always selects the card the
# player is pointing at, measured in real pixels in headless Chrome.
!test_hand_limit_discard.py
!test_hand_discard_click.js
# Playing on a phone: the in-game screen laid the player seats out flanking the
# hand in a grid whose side columns never shrink, so on a phone the hand's own
# column collapsed to zero and the whole fan spilled underneath the seat pills
# (which take the tap), every card in your hand was unclickable, two were off
# the screen entirely, and the deck you draw from sat past the right edge.
!test_mobile_layout.js
# Weekly Challenges: daily challenges were retired (1.6.54), and the weekly
# strip now starts CLOSED. Proves the daily system left no stumps behind, no
# pool, no state, no reporting hook, and none of the per-payload observer state
# that only ever fed one, and measures the closed/open strip in headless
# Chrome, because "hidden until you click it" is a thing only the browser knows.
!test_weekly_challenges.js
# Tutorials: walks the Main Menu and Competitive tours through the REAL app in
# headless Chrome, as a guest AND signed in, at five widths, pressing Next
# when it is offered and otherwise clicking whatever the spotlight is on. It
# catches the class of bug a source grep cannot: every selector valid, every
# handler wired, and the running tour still dead-ends (the History step used to
# demand three clicks inside a modal its own popup was covering).
!test_tutorials.js
# Staying up under a full house: the bot planner's CPU admission control (32
# concurrent games used to drive a request that touches nothing from 2.5 ms to
# 5.3 seconds) and the room janitor that stops finished rooms living forever in
# memory and on the mounted disk.
!test_server_load_safety.py
# Competitive: one human owns TWO seats, so the turn must reach both of their
# hands. The .js half proves the client follows the turn to its other hand (the
# per-seat render gate); the .py half runs a real 4-seat match and covers the
# player-vs-seat rules, Play Again, leaving, Surf's Up.
!test_competitive_hand_switch.js
!test_competitive_mode.py
# Dragging cards around inside your hand is client-only state, and it used to
# live in ONE array, so drawing your OTHER competitive hand deleted the first
# hand's arrangement. This runs the real ordering lines, lifted out of
# preview-app.js, over a player who owns two seats.
!test_competitive_hand_order.js
# Spectating: a spectator has no seat, and a player's equipped icon used to
# travel only on their seat, so watchers showed up as a bare name and their
# chat lines wore a hash of "[Spectator] Name" instead of the icon they equipped.
!test_spectator_avatar.py
!test_spectator_avatar.js
# In-game faces: changing your avatar mid-game has to change it on every
# surface at once (seats, chat lines, draw notifications, final standings),
# not just the one that happens to re-read the state.
!test_ingame_avatar_live.js

# The published How to Play page (/rules): proves the rulebook stylesheet and
# the shared data tables were MOVED out of the game, not copied (it compares
# them against the previous commit), and that every asset the page names
# resolves on both the Render game server and the Vercel marketing site.
!test_rules_page.js

# The Player Home left sidebar. It is its own scroller, so the white nav card
# inside it has to be as tall as its own content, otherwise you scroll down to
# the Daily Streak block and the card has already ended above it, leaving "View
# streak details" on the bare page. Measured in headless Chrome at nine window
# sizes (including the Streak Rescue worst case), and again along the path the
# Main Menu tutorial takes to those same two elements.
!test_sidebar_scroll.js

# The Stripe money path. The .py half covers the server's decision functions , 
# webhook signature verification (the only thing between a stranger's POST and
# free coins), price→reward mapping, and the lifetime-total wall tiers. The .js
# half runs the REAL /thanks page in a stubbed DOM: the back-to-game button (an
# untrusted cross-origin value, so it must never become an open redirect) and
# the rule that it only claims "confirmed" when the SERVER says so.
!test_stripe_payments.py
# Asks Stripe what the 7 live Payment Links actually charge and ask for, the
# half of the money path the test suite can't see. Dev tool, never in the image.
!verify_stripe_links.py
# Guards the privacy page: one source for the document, both /privacy spellings,
# and the ?v= stamps on every asset it and preview.html share, all compared
# against version.json, so a build bump can't leave a stale stamp behind.
!test_privacy_policy.js
!test_thanks_page.js
# The two screens you click before you have an account. The device gate and the
# sign-in chooser paint their buttons in the same place, so choosing a device
# used to leave the cursor sitting on an invisible PLAY AS GUEST. Measured in a
# real browser at six window sizes, no source grep can see it.
!test_auth_gate.js
# CREATE YOUR USERNAME. The kelp backdrop cannot live inside the card it
# backs (a negative-z child paints over its own parent's background), and
# Chrome will not open a window under 500px, so the phone widths are
# measured in iframes. Neither fact survives a source grep.
!test_username_screen.js
# PLAY AS A GUEST. The step it lives in paints two INVISIBLE buttons, so its
# rules say background/color:transparent and position:absolute; as a descendant
# selector they reached into the guest card and erased Dive In. That, the fixed
# scrim, and the phone widths are all measurements, not greppable source.
!test_guest_card.js
# SIGN IN / CREATE AN ACCOUNT: the second way in, a username and a password,
# for the people who have no Google account. The half that cannot be grepped is
# the measured half: that the two boxes a player clicks really LAND on the two
# buttons painted into login-bg.png at every window size, that a phone-shaped
# window crops the printed octagon away entirely before redrawing one, that both
# labels fit inside their buttons, that Create Account is above the fold on a
# laptop, and that the green strength bar and the button it gates agree at the
# exact score they change on.
!test_account_signin.js
# One-shot image surgery: CURRENTS & CRITTERS was painted 46px right of the
# middle of login-bg.png, and this is what moved it. Kept because the next
# redraw of that artwork will probably need it again and the awkward parts (the
# hard seam between the two top panels runs through the "&", and the reef at
# the right-hand end is diagonal) took several tries to get right.
!tools_center_signin_title.py
# The rotted pier piling: the plank hidden in the sign-in artwork, and the Pier
# background it is worth. The measured half reads the PAINTING back out of a
# canvas: the pixel under the middle of the box has to be rust red, at every
# size, so an art rebuild that moves the pier fails here instead of quietly
# leaving the secret over open water. The rest is the rule that makes it a
# reward and not a party trick: nothing is granted on the sign-in screen because
# nobody is there yet, and the find is spent on ONE account, once, whenever that
# account turns up.
!test_pier_piling.js
# The sign-in screen's status line, and the full-screen chip. What it pins is
# all measurement: that the note is a card and not red text, that it never eats
# a click meant for the artwork, that a calm note takes itself away, and that
# the corner chip cannot appear before body.cc-signed-in exists.
!test_auth_notice.js
# Player Home's four action cards and the Friends tab. It measures what a
# stylesheet read cannot: that each card's NAME is light ink on its gradient
# (a stale second .ph-action-label rule paints it navy, which was invisible
# while the cards were images), and that the reef is attached to the window.
!test_home_actions.js
# THE FULL-SCREEN TOGGLE on the menu. Runs the real setupGameWindowFullscreen()
# sliced out of preview-app.js against the real stylesheet, at five widths and
# on six tabs, and measures the three things a source read cannot answer: that
# the chip lands in the BOTTOM-RIGHT corner, that its rectangle is identical on
# every tab, and that nothing on any of those tabs is painted over it.
!test_fullscreen_chip.js

!fish_ai_brain.json
!multiplayer/
!multiplayer/client/
!multiplayer/client/**
!multiplayer/human_game_dataset.jsonl
!multiplayer/tunnel_pool.py
!cards_vertical.txt
!cards_lr.txt
!cards_oceans.txt
!update_cards.py

# Recovery + learning tools (run locally, never commit serviceAccountKey.json)
!apply_recovery.js
!apply_history_learning.js
serviceAccountKey.json

# Snap & Score local card recognition: build-time library generator + tests
# (the generated snap-card-library.json ships inside multiplayer/client/).
!build_snap_card_library.py
!test_snap_vision.js
!test_snap_vision_fixtures.json
!test_snap_local_board.py
!make_snap_test_photo.py

# Streak leaderboard (Leaderboard tab → 🔥 Streak)
!test_streak_leaderboard.js
# The "play again" callout: a Hermit Crab / Sea Turtle / ★ extra turn is
# announced by the turn banner at the TOP of the screen while the action it
# asks for is at the BOTTOM, so players sat on an extra turn thinking the game
# had stalled. Measures the callout above the action bar in headless Chrome , 
# on screen, pointing at End Turn, and covering no other control even when the
# bar wraps onto three rows on a phone.
!test_play_again_callout.js
# …and WHICH cards may raise it. A Hermit Crab / Loggerhead Sea Turtle opens a
# window you have to close yourself, so the turn sits there looking frozen, a
# Roosterfish's one free Baitfish does not, and must not prompt. Runs every
# card in the shipped deck files through the real ability executor, so the line
# is drawn by card TEXT, not by a hand-written list of names.
!test_end_turn_callout_cards.py
# The end screen keeps trying: once a game is over its state_version stops
# changing, so the payload is dropped as already-rendered and renderEndGame , 
# the only caller of saveGameStats, is never reached again. One second of lost
# network therefore cost the player their XP, streak and game history on a
# screen that showed all three. Pins the watchdog and the three save states.
!test_endgame_save_retry.js
# Game Night pays 1.75x XP, and the whole feature is "at the right hour": the
# schedule and the multiplier live in ONE module, so this sweeps a year of
# Saturdays a minute at a time (DST included) and runs the app's real prestigeXp
# to pin the stacking order and the never-below-1x fallbacks.
!test_game_night_xp.js
# A guest gets the whole game, minus the saving. Walks the real app in a real
# browser as a signed-out player: every one of the 13 tabs opens and renders
# real content, the Avatar Gallery opens with the 6 PAID critters locked and
# the earnable ones wearable, and the device is detected (touch = mobile,
# mouse = computer) with no "choose your device" screen anywhere.
!test_guest_access.js

# Newsletter tests + setup guide + the one-off OAuth helper.
# (newsletter_server.py / newsletter_email.py are allowlisted further up, next
# to the other server modules.) NOTE: .gitignore and .dockerignore are BOTH
# `*`-then-`!` allowlists, so a new root file is silently excluded from git AND
# from the image until it is named in both. Verify with: git check-ignore -v <f>
# The helper script and the tests are dev tools, deliberately NOT in the image.
!test_newsletter_server.py
!test_newsletter_admin_ui.js
!NEWSLETTER_SETUP.md
!scripts/
!scripts/get_gmail_refresh_token.py

# Admin hand-out: Clan Points to a clan + the matching XP to the member.
# Run locally against Firestore with GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS, a dev
# tool, deliberately NOT in the image.
!scripts/grant_clan_points.py

# Player Perks (Store: Streak Shield / Name Token / Emote Pack / Re-Earn)
!test_store_perks.js
!test_chat_emotes.py

